    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Schubert Dip is the debut album by British band EMF, released on Parlophone in 1991, and it blends electronic production with rock energy in a lively, club-friendly style. The album’s title is a pun on the sweet “sherbet dip” and composer Franz Schubert, which suits EMF’s playful mix of dance beats, guitar hooks, and cheeky pop appeal.\n\nThe record’s best-known track is “Unbelievable,” a crossover hit that became a worldwide anthem and reached number one on the US Billboard Hot 100. The album also helped establish EMF as part of the early-1990s wave of British alternative dance and indie rock, with songs such as “Children,” “I Believe,” and “Lies” reinforcing its mix of punchy rhythms, melodic choruses, and rave-era swagger.",
